\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Mixed-area formulas connect stable intersection multiplicity of plane tropical curves with the convex geometry of their Newton polygons. Their numerical form depends essentially on the convention used for area. This paper isolates the normalization issue for the standard Newton triangles of curves of degrees $d$ and $e$. If normalized lattice area assigns area $1$ to a primitive lattice triangle, then the raw Minkowski polarization is
\[
 A((d+e)\DeltaStd)-A(d\DeltaStd)-A(e\DeltaStd)=2de,
\]
not $de$. Consequently, the raw normalized-area difference disagrees with the plane B\'ezout number for every pair of positive degrees; two tropical lines give the minimal counterexample, with area difference $2$ and intersection number $1$. The corrected convention divides the polarization by $2$. Equivalently, Euclidean area may be used without that factor. We prove the degree-triangle identity, positive-degree incompatibility of the uncorrected formula, symmetry, and additivity, and give exact procedures for auditing these quantities. The results fix the normalization required for a general polygonal mixed-area intersection theory.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Tropical geometry translates algebraic data into piecewise-linear and polyhedral geometry. For a polynomial in two variables, its exponent vectors determine a Newton polygon. The associated tropical curve is a balanced weighted polyhedral complex in the plane, and intersections of such curves carry local multiplicities. A tropical analogue of B\'ezout's theorem relates total stable intersection multiplicity to convex-geometric information derived from Newton polygons.

For convex polygons $P$ and $Q$, their Minkowski sum is
\[
 P+Q=\{p+q:p\in P,\ q\in Q\}.
\]
A mixed term is extracted from the quadratic behavior of area by considering
\[
 A(P+Q)-A(P)-A(Q).
\]
Its exact relation to intersection multiplicity depends on the scale of $A$. In the plane, normalized lattice area is twice Euclidean area. A formula written for Euclidean area therefore acquires a factor $1/2$ when rewritten using normalized lattice area.

The issue is completely visible for the standard triangle
\[
 \DeltaStd=\conv\{(0,0),(1,0),(0,1)\}.
\]
The dilation $d\DeltaStd$ is the standard Newton polygon for degree $d$. With normalized lattice area, $A(\DeltaStd)=1$ and $A(d\DeltaStd)=d^2$. Since $d\DeltaStd+e\DeltaStd=(d+e)\DeltaStd$, direct expansion gives
\[
 A((d+e)\DeltaStd)-A(d\DeltaStd)-A(e\DeltaStd)=2de.
\]
Thus an assertion that the normalized-area difference itself equals $de$ is false. For $d,e>0$, it is always larger by $de$. The corrected expression is
\[
 I(P,Q)=\frac{A(P+Q)-A(P)-A(Q)}{2}.
\]
Our aim is to state the conventions explicitly, derive the exact identities, and formulate structural and computational checks that prevent normalization errors.

\section{Geometric preliminaries}

\subsection{Area conventions}

\begin{definition}[Lattice polygon]
A lattice polygon is a convex polygon in $\mathbb R^2$ whose vertices lie in $\mathbb Z^2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Euclidean and normalized lattice area]
The ordinary area of a plane region $P$ is denoted by $\EArea(P)$. For a lattice polygon, its normalized lattice area is
\[
 A(P)=2\EArea(P).
\]
Thus $\EArea(\DeltaStd)=1/2$ and $A(\DeltaStd)=1$.
\end{definition}

The factor $2$ is the planar instance of normalization by $n!$ in dimension $n$. It makes the area of a primitive lattice triangle integral.

\begin{definition}[Standard degree triangle]
For an integer $d\geq 0$, set
\[
 d\DeltaStd=\{dx:x\in\DeltaStd\}
 =\conv\{(0,0),(d,0),(0,d)\}.
\]
This is the standard Newton triangle of degree $d$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Area scaling]\label{lem:scaling}
For every integer $d\geq0$,
\[
 A(d\DeltaStd)=d^2.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Dilation by $d$ multiplies two-dimensional Euclidean area by $d^2$. Hence $\EArea(d\DeltaStd)=d^2/2$, and multiplication by $2$ gives normalized area $d^2$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Minkowski addition}

\begin{definition}[Minkowski sum]
For $P,Q\subseteq\mathbb R^2$, define
\[
 P+Q=\{p+q:p\in P,\ q\in Q\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Addition of homothetic triangles]\label{lem:minkowski}
For integers $d,e\geq0$,
\[
 d\DeltaStd+e\DeltaStd=(d+e)\DeltaStd.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Suppose first that $d+e>0$. If $x,y\in\DeltaStd$, convexity gives
\[
 \frac d{d+e}x+\frac e{d+e}y\in\DeltaStd.
\]
Multiplying by $d+e$ shows that $dx+ey\in(d+e)\DeltaStd$. Conversely, for $z\in\DeltaStd$, one has $(d+e)z=dz+ez$, with $dz\in d\DeltaStd$ and $ez\in e\DeltaStd$. The case $d=e=0$ is immediate.
\end{proof}

\section{Raw polarization and corrected mixed area}

\begin{definition}[Raw normalized-area polarization]
For integers $d,e\geq0$, define
\[
 D(d,e)=A((d+e)\DeltaStd)-A(d\DeltaStd)-A(e\DeltaStd).
\]
The word ``raw'' indicates that no compensation has been made for the scale of normalized area.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Degree-triangle polarization identity]\label{thm:twice}
For all integers $d,e\geq0$,
\[
 D(d,e)=2de.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:scaling},
\[
 D(d,e)=(d+e)^2-d^2-e^2.
\]
Expanding and cancelling gives
\[
 d^2+2de+e^2-d^2-e^2=2de.
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Corrected normalized mixed area]
For lattice polygons $P$ and $Q$, define the corrected polarization by
\[
 I_A(P,Q)=\frac{A(P+Q)-A(P)-A(Q)}2.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{corollary}[Corrected degree B\'ezout identity]\label{cor:bezout}
For integers $d,e\geq0$,
\[
 I_A(d\DeltaStd,e\DeltaStd)=de.
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
The numerator is $2de$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:twice}; division by $2$ gives the claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Equivalence of area conventions]\label{prop:equiv}
For lattice polygons $P$ and $Q$,
\[
 I_A(P,Q)=\EArea(P+Q)-\EArea(P)-\EArea(Q).
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Substitute $A(R)=2\EArea(R)$ for $R=P+Q,P,Q$ into the definition of $I_A$ and cancel the common factor $2$.
\end{proof}

Accordingly, one may either use Euclidean area and the unhalved difference, or normalized lattice area and half the difference. These conventions are mathematically equivalent when used consistently.

\section{Counterexample and positive-degree obstruction}

\begin{theorem}[Two-line counterexample]\label{thm:lines}
For $d=e=1$, the raw normalized-area polarization is
\[
 D(1,1)=2,
\]
and hence is not equal to the line--line B\'ezout number $1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The normalized areas are $A(2\DeltaStd)=4$ and $A(\DeltaStd)=1$. Therefore
\[
 D(1,1)=4-1-1=2\neq1.
\]
\end{proof}

Two generic plane tropical lines have total stable intersection multiplicity $1$, and both have Newton polygon $\DeltaStd$. Thus any polygonal formula for their intersection count must return $1$ on $(\DeltaStd,\DeltaStd)$. The raw normalized polarization returns $2$; the corrected expression returns $1$.

\begin{theorem}[Positive-degree incompatibility]\label{thm:positive}
If $d>0$ and $e>0$, then
\[
 D(d,e)\neq de.
\]
More precisely,
\[
 D(d,e)-de=de>0.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:twice} gives $D(d,e)=2de$, and therefore $D(d,e)-de=de$. Positivity of both degrees implies $de>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Accidental equality]
For integers $d,e\geq0$, the equality $D(d,e)=de$ holds if and only if $d=0$ or $e=0$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:twice}, the asserted equality is equivalent to $2de=de$, hence to $de=0$. Over the nonnegative integers, this occurs exactly when one factor vanishes.
\end{proof}

The zero-degree cases merely make both quantities zero. Every nontrivial positive-degree pair detects the normalization discrepancy.

\section{Structural properties}

\begin{theorem}[Symmetry]\label{thm:symmetry}
For integers $d,e\geq0$,
\[
 D(d,e)=D(e,d),
\qquad
 I_A(d\DeltaStd,e\DeltaStd)=I_A(e\DeltaStd,d\DeltaStd).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:twice} gives $D(d,e)=2de=2ed=D(e,d)$. Division by $2$ proves the second identity.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Additivity]\label{thm:additivity}
For integers $a,b,e\geq0$,
\[
 D(a+b,e)=D(a,e)+D(b,e).
\]
Consequently,
\[
 I_A((a+b)\DeltaStd,e\DeltaStd)
 =I_A(a\DeltaStd,e\DeltaStd)+I_A(b\DeltaStd,e\DeltaStd).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:twice},
\[
 D(a+b,e)=2(a+b)e=2ae+2be=D(a,e)+D(b,e).
\]
Dividing by $2$ proves the corrected identity.
\end{proof}

By symmetry, additivity also holds in the second argument. Hence $(d,e)\mapsto I_A(d\DeltaStd,e\DeltaStd)$ is a symmetric biadditive function, explicitly equal to $de$.

The factor $2$ has the same origin as the standard polarization identity. If $q(u)=B(u,u)$ for a symmetric bilinear form $B$, then
\[
 q(u+v)-q(u)-q(v)=2B(u,v).
\]
Here $d\mapsto d^2$ is the quadratic area function and multiplication $(d,e)\mapsto de$ is its associated bilinear form.

\section{Exact computational procedures}

\begin{algorithm}[Degree-triangle normalization audit]\label{alg:audit}
Given nonnegative integer degrees $d$ and $e$:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
 \item Compute $A_d=d^2$ and $A_e=e^2$.
 \item Compute $A_{d+e}=(d+e)^2$.
 \item Form $D=A_{d+e}-A_d-A_e$.
 \item Form the corrected mixed area $I=D/2$.
 \item Compute the B\'ezout product $B=de$.
 \item Report $D$, $I$, and $B$, and test whether $D=B$ and $I=B$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{algorithm}

The procedure uses exact integer arithmetic. It performs a constant number of additions, multiplications, and divisions, hence $O(1)$ arithmetic operations. If the inputs have at most $n$ bits, ordinary multiplication yields $O(n^2)$ bit complexity and the storage requirement is $O(n)$ bits.

\begin{proposition}[Correctness of the audit]
For every input pair $(d,e)$, Algorithm~\ref{alg:audit} returns
\[
 D=2de,\qquad I=de,\qquad B=de.
\]
If both degrees are positive, it certifies $D\neq B$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The first identity is Theorem~\ref{thm:twice}; exact division gives the second, and the third is the definition of $B$. The final assertion is Theorem~\ref{thm:positive}.
\end{proof}

An additivity audit takes $(a,b,e)$, compares $D(a+b,e)$ with $D(a,e)+D(b,e)$, and repeats the comparison after division by $2$. Theorem~\ref{thm:additivity} guarantees equality. This structural test is valuable because a candidate expression can match isolated numerical examples while failing mixed-area behavior.

For degree triangles, $D(d,e)=2de$ is always even. Corrected mixed area is therefore computed by exact integer division, without floating-point arithmetic or rounding. For arbitrary lattice polygons, the shoelace formula naturally computes twice Euclidean area as an integer; this is exactly normalized lattice area. A general polygonal implementation can form the normalized polarization, verify parity, and divide by $2$.

\section{Worked examples}

The closed formulas permit exact evaluation without geometric approximation. We record examples displaying normalization, zero-degree behavior, and additivity.

\subsection{A line and a quartic}

For $d=1$ and $e=4$,
\[
 A(\DeltaStd)=1,\qquad A(4\DeltaStd)=16,\qquad A(5\DeltaStd)=25.
\]
Thus $D(1,4)=25-1-16=8$, while corrected mixed area is $8/2=4$, equal to the B\'ezout product $1\cdot4$.

\subsection{A conic and a cubic}

For $d=2$ and $e=3$,
\[
 A(2\DeltaStd)=4,\qquad A(3\DeltaStd)=9,\qquad A(5\DeltaStd)=25.
\]
Hence $D(2,3)=25-4-9=12$, and the corrected value is $6=2\cdot3$. The discrepancy is therefore not a fixed additive error: it is the B\'ezout product itself.

\subsection{A vanishing degree}

If $d=0$ and $e=5$, then
\[
 D(0,5)=A(5\DeltaStd)-A(0\DeltaStd)-A(5\DeltaStd)=0.
\]
Both raw polarization and degree product vanish. Tests involving a zero degree therefore cannot expose the normalization mistake; positive inputs are essential.

\subsection{Decomposing a degree}

Take $a=2$, $b=3$, and $e=4$. Directly, $D(5,4)=40$, whereas
\[
 D(2,4)+D(3,4)=16+24=40.
\]
After halving, the corrected values are $20$ and $8+12=20$. Decomposing a degree decomposes its mixed interaction exactly.

\subsection{A geometric dissection}

A square of side $d+e$ can be cut into a $d$-by-$d$ square, an $e$-by-$e$ square, and two $d$-by-$e$ rectangles. Removing the pure square areas leaves two rectangles of area $de$, hence a remainder of $2de$. This dissection visually explains why raw polarization of a quadratic function contains two copies of its symmetric mixed term.

\section{Applications and interpretation}

\subsection{Tropical B\'ezout counting}

For standard degree triangles, corrected mixed area equals $de$, the expected total stable intersection multiplicity for plane tropical curves of degrees $d$ and $e$ under the usual hypotheses. The triangle computation does not itself construct stable intersections or establish the full polygonal intersection theorem. It instead proves which normalization any such theorem must use in the degree case.

A full tropical B\'ezout theorem combines balancedness, stable perturbation, determinant-valued local multiplicities, and a global conservation law. Once expressed using normalized lattice area, that theorem must contain the factor $1/2$ identified here.

\subsection{Sparse polynomial systems}

Newton polygons encode the monomial supports of bivariate polynomials. Mixed-area quantities can count isolated solutions in an algebraic torus under suitable nondegeneracy hypotheses. Since changing area conventions rescales these quantities, an explicit convention prevents a systematic doubling of predicted solution counts.

\subsection{Computational pipelines}

Geometry libraries often expose Euclidean polygon area, whereas combinatorial packages may expose normalized lattice area or twice signed area. Combining these values without conversion silently creates the discrepancy analyzed above. The pair $(d,e)=(1,1)$ is an effective unit test: the raw normalized difference must be $2$, while the corrected count must be $1$.

\subsection{Polarization as a general method}

The calculation exemplifies a broad principle. A homogeneous quadratic measurement contains a bilinear interaction term, and polarization extracts it. The coefficient depends on convention. Normalized lattice area makes primitive lattice triangles integral but doubles Euclidean area; consequently, its raw cross term is twice the desired mixed quantity. This derivation is more robust than memorizing a correction factor.

\section{Scope and limitations}

The results concern the standard one-parameter family of triangular Newton polygons. They determine normalization for degree-based plane formulas, but do not alone establish a tropical B\'ezout theorem for arbitrary polygons. Claims about transverse intersections, generic perturbations, and sums of local determinant multiplicities require additional geometric arguments.

The restriction to nonnegative integral degrees ensures that $d\DeltaStd$ and $e\DeltaStd$ are lattice polygons and that $de$ is an integral count. Although the polarization identity extends algebraically to real scaling parameters, its interpretation as a curve-intersection count belongs to the lattice setting.

Terminology also varies: some conventions call $V(P,Q)$ the mixed area when
\[
 \EArea(sP+tQ)=s^2\EArea(P)+2stV(P,Q)+t^2\EArea(Q),
\]
whereas others attach the name to the full cross term. The reliable practice is to state the formula explicitly and calibrate it at $P=Q=\DeltaStd$. Under the present convention, corrected normalized mixed area returns $1$ on that primitive pair.

\section{Discussion}

The principal lesson is that ``normalized'' does not mean ``coefficient-free.'' Normalized lattice area is useful precisely because it assigns area $1$ to a primitive triangle. The same choice rescales every planar area difference by $2$ relative to Euclidean area. Since the B\'ezout number remains $de$, conversion must occur in the formula.

The degree-triangle case is a complete diagnostic. Its three ingredients are explicit:
\[
 A(d\DeltaStd)=d^2,
 \qquad d\DeltaStd+e\DeltaStd=(d+e)\DeltaStd,
\]
and
\[
 A((d+e)\DeltaStd)-A(d\DeltaStd)-A(e\DeltaStd)=2de.
\]
No geometric degeneracy or local multiplicity convention can alter this algebraic identity. Once normalized area is fixed, half-polarization is forced.

Symmetry and additivity show that the correction is structurally natural. The raw expression is already symmetric and biadditive in degree parameters, but its scale is twice the intended one. Multiplication by $1/2$ preserves those structures and calibrates the invariant against the primitive line--line intersection.

\section{Future work}

The next step is to pass from homothetic triangles to arbitrary finite lattice polygons. One must define exact normalized area and prove that the area of Minkowski combinations is a homogeneous quadratic polynomial. Half-polarization should then yield a symmetric, Minkowski-additive mixed-area invariant.

A second step is to connect that invariant to balanced tropical curves. The desired theorem identifies determinant-weighted total multiplicity of a finite stable intersection with corrected mixed area of the Newton polygons. Perturbation invariance should ensure that sufficiently small generic translations preserve the total weighted count.

Further questions concern support bounds under positive local multiplicities, comparison with realizable curves over complete non-Archimedean fields, and higher-dimensional analogues. For $n$ transverse tropical hypersurfaces of degrees $d_1,\ldots,d_n$ in tropical projective $n$-space, the expected weighted zero-dimensional count is
\[
 \prod_{i=1}^n d_i.
\]
In higher dimensions, normalization by $n!$ and conventions for mixed volume must be specified as carefully as the planar factor $2$.

\section{Conclusion}

For standard Newton triangles measured by normalized lattice area, raw polarization is exactly $2de$. This gives a minimal counterexample to the uncorrected formula at $(1,1)$ and proves incompatibility with $de$ for every positive degree pair. Dividing by $2$ restores the plane B\'ezout value while preserving symmetry and additivity. Equivalently, Euclidean area may be used without division. A mixed-area tropical B\'ezout formula must therefore declare its area convention, and normalized lattice area requires the missing half.

The broader methodological point is equally direct. Before using a convex-geometric invariant as an enumerative count, one should calibrate it on primitive objects whose intersections are already understood. Here the pair of degree-one triangles distinguishes the two consistent area conventions, rules out the inconsistent hybrid, and supplies a reusable test for later generalizations.

